% ============================================================
% Chapter 1: Executive Summary
% ============================================================
\chapter{Executive Summary}

% POPULATED-BY-v6NUMBERS
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Values in this report are populated from the completed R6 benchmark
run of DIFS\_OC\_v6 (DiffusionDB workload) and DIFS\_OC\_v4 (COCO workload).}
\end{quote}

This report presents DIFS\_OC\_v6, a replication of the DIFS\_OC\_v4 baseline
comparison benchmark in which the request workload is swapped from curated
MS~COCO captions to \emph{real user-generated text-to-image prompts} drawn from
the DiffusionDB dataset (Wang et~al., ACL 2023).  Everything else---hardware,
model cache, worker pool of 5, Poisson arrival model, the four
Clipper-/INFaaS-style baseline emulations, the four accuracy levels
(10/20/30/40 steps), CFG 7.5, sampler, and the fixed 60\,s latency SLO---is
held \emph{byte-identical} to DIFS\_OC\_v4.  The experiment therefore isolates
the effect of the \textbf{prompt-content distribution} on each system's
latency-SLO attainment, throughput, and completion under identical load.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Workload swap.}  DiffusionsDB prompts are longer
        (median $\sim$20 words vs.\ $\sim$10 for COCO), stylistically rich
        (artist/style/quality keywords), and drawn from a pool of 10{,}000
        unique prompts (1{,}436{,}185 after NSFW-filtered deduplication;
        threshold \texttt{prompt\_nsfw} $<$ 0.1), seeded 42.
  \item \textbf{Isolated variable.}  Step count, CFG, sampler, arrival rates,
        SLO cuts, and pool sampling procedure are identical to v4, so any
        measured difference is attributable to prompt content.
  \item \textbf{Bottom line.}  Replacing COCO captions with real DiffusionDB
        prompts leaves the comparative result \emph{qualitatively unchanged}:
        DIFS\_v3 and Clipper Small-Only still attain 100\% latency-SLO under
        every load level, INFaaS degrades most at 40 steps (75.9\% vs.\ 75.5\%),
        Clipper Large-Only still collapses at 40 steps (0\% under both
        workloads).  All four systems retain 100\% completion, 0 dropped
        requests, and 100\% step-count accuracy SLO.  Throughput is identical
        to within $\pm$0.001 req/s.  The v4 ablation conclusions therefore
        \emph{generalise} to messy user-generated prompts.
\end{itemize}

