% ============================================================
% Chapter 2: Distinguishing DIFS_OC_v6
% ============================================================
\chapter{Distinguishing DIFS\_OC\_v6}

\section{DIFS\_OC\_v6 vs.\ DIFS\_OC\_v4}

DIFS\_OC\_v4 and DIFS\_OC\_v6 are the two halves of a controlled
\emph{workload-generalisation} study on the same hardware and codebase with the
same baseline emulations:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Identical.} Both operate on a single RTX 6000 Ada (49\,GB) node,
        use the same model cache (SD-v1-5, SD-2-1, SDXL-Base, SD-3.5-Medium,
        SD-3.5-Large), a fixed pool of 5 workers, the same four baseline
        configurations (Clipper-style Large-Only, Clipper-style Small-Only,
        INFaaS-style, DIFS\_OC\_v3), Poisson arrivals at the five load levels
        (2/4/6/8/10 req/min), a fixed 60\,s latency SLO, step-count accuracy
        SLO (10/20/30/40), CFG 7.5, sampler, and the same seed-42 deterministic
        prompt sampling procedure (one prompt per request).
  \item \textbf{Different.} Only the \textbf{prompt source} changes:
        v4 samples 300 of 202{,}654 COCO captions per level; v6 samples 300 of
        10{,}000 DiffusionDB prompts per level.  No other code path, metric
        definition, or environment variable differs.
  \item \textbf{Purpose.} v4 answered ``how do the four systems compare under
        a curated caption workload?''; v6 answers ``\emph{do those conclusions
        generalise when the workload is the messy, long, stylised, real-user
        prompt text that a production text-to-image service actually
        encounters?}''.
\end{itemize}

\section{DIFS\_OC\_v6 vs.\ the earlier DIFS projects (in general)}

\begin{description}
  \item[DIFS\_OC\_v1/v2 --- INFaaS lineage.] Direct descendants of the INFaaS
        codebase (Romero et~al., ATC~2021); established the single-node
        multi-model diffusion worker pool, the gRPC scheduler protocol, and the
        model registry used in every later project.
  \item[DIFS\_OC\_v3 --- the multiworker SLO benchmark.] Introduced EDF
        scheduling, locality-aware routing, and fair-share step adaptation, and
        produced the first SLO-guarantee results.  Its \texttt{src/} is the
        basis for v4 and v6.
  \item[DIFS\_OC\_v4 --- baseline comparison.]  Benchmarked v3 against
        Clipper-/INFaaS-\emph{emulations} on the \textbf{COCO} caption workload
        (this session's reference set: Clipper, INFaaS, MuxServe, WarmServe).
  \item[DIFS\_OC\_v5 --- switching-modes study.] Measured hot-switch,
        forced-DRAM-reload, and cold reload costs on the same GPU; did not
        change the workload.
  \item[DIFS\_OC\_v6 --- \textbf{this project}.] Reuses the v4 harness
        unchanged but replaces the workload with real DiffusionDB user prompts,
        so the v4 conclusions can be stress-tested against a distribution
        shift in the input text.
\end{description}

Put differently: \textbf{v6 is the first DIFS project in which the \emph{workload
itself} is the experimental variable}, rather than the scheduling policy, model
pool, or infrastructure.

\section{Why the DiffusionDB dataset}

DiffusionDB (Wang et~al., ACL 2023, arXiv:2210.14896) is a large-scale
dataset of \textbf{real user-generated}\footnote{collected from the official
Stable Diffusion Discord bot, August 2022} text-to-image prompts.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Scale.} 2M images / 1.4M unique prompts in the 2M subset
        ($\sim$14M / 1.8M in the \emph{large} version).
  \item \textbf{Realism.}  Prompts are authentic production text --- long
        comma/tag-packed strings with artist names, style and quality
        modifiers, and idiosyncratic phrasing --- unlike curated,
        short descriptive COCO sentences.
  \item \textbf{Distribution shift.}  Median length $\sim$20 words vs.\ $\sim$8--12
        for COCO; >50\% of pool prompts are $\geq$20 words; heavy-length tail.
  \item \textbf{Safety-aware.}  Per-prompt NSFW scores
        (\texttt{prompt\_nsfw}, \texttt{image\_nsfw}) allow principled
        filtering ($<$0.1 used here).
  \item \textbf{External validity.}  Because requests resemble real user
        traffic, v6 results speak to the behaviour a deployed diffusion-SLO
        system would exhibit, strengthening the generality of the v4 ablation.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Chapter 3: Experimental Setup
% ============================================================

\chapter{Experimental Setup}

\section{Hardware}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GPU:} NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada (49\,GB).
  \item \textbf{Worker pool:} 5 workers, one vanilla diffusion model per worker,
        launched as direct Python subprocesses (no Docker).
  \item \textbf{Models} ($id$: name): 3=SD-v1-5, 4=SD-2-1, 5=SDXL-Base,
        8=SD-3.5-Medium, 9=SD-3.5-Large.  Cache:
        \texttt{/mnt/ssd4t/.../downloaded\_diffusion\_models/diffusion-models}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Workload: DiffusionDB prompt pool}

Built by \texttt{src/scripts/build\_diffusiondb\_pool.py}:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Download \texttt{poloclub/diffusiondb} \texttt{metadata.parquet}
        (2{,}000{,}000 rows).
  \item Drop rows with \texttt{prompt\_nsfw} $< 0.1$ $\to$ 1{,}887{,}112 kept.
  \item Deduplicate trimmed prompt text (first-occurrence) $\to$ 1{,}436{,}185
        unique prompts.
  \item Deterministically sample 10{,}000 (seeded 42).
  \item Serialise as COCO-schema JSON
        (\texttt{annotations/diffusiondb\_prompts.json}) so the v4 client loader
        \texttt{extract\_coco\_prompts()} works unchanged.
\end{enumerate}

Prompt-stat summary: min 1 word, median 20, max 160; $\geq$20-word fraction
51.4\%.

\section{Workload: parity with v4}

Each SLO-benchmark request sends \texttt{-prompt\_count 1} with
\texttt{DIFS\_PROMPTS\_FILE} pointing at the DiffusionDB JSON; selection uses
seed 42 exactly as v4 did for COCO.  All other constants come from
\texttt{src/experiments/experiment\_config.py} unchanged (see CH~1 of the v4
report for the full list).

\section{Metrics}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Latency SLO attainment} (\%): fraction of requests whose
        end-to-end latency $<$ fixed 60{,}000 ms budget.
  \item \textbf{Accuracy SLO attainment} (\%): fraction of completed requests
        served at or above the required step count for their accuracy level
        ($\sigma(\mathrm{low})=10$, $\sigma(\mathrm{medium})=20$,
        $\sigma(\mathrm{high})=30$, $\sigma(\mathrm{very\_high})=40$).  Because
        every completed request is resident at its own required step count,
        this is 100\% for all systems (real differentiators are latency SLO and
        throughput).
  \item \textbf{Throughput} (req/s): completed requests per second.
  \item \textbf{Completion} (\%): requests\_completed / requests\_submitted per
        cell.
\end{itemize}

