\section{Interpretation}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{The latency-SLO cliff does not shift.} The step-count cliff
        locations are identical under both workloads.  Clipper Large-Only hits
        0\% latency-SLO attainment at 40 steps in both the COCO run (0.0) and the
        DiffusionDB run (0.0); its 30-step cell is 97.1\% (COCO) vs.\ 93.0\%
        (DiffusionDB), a small move driven by the low-load cell's Poisson sample
        (41 vs.\ 42 requests).  INFaaS degrades only at 40 steps: 75.5\%
        (COCO) vs.\ 75.9\% (DiffusionDB)---statistically unchanged.  The longer,
        more stylised DiffusionDB prompt text therefore does \emph{not} shift
        the SLO cliff in either direction.
  \item \textbf{Per-step attainment patterns are preserved.} Clipper Small-Only
        and DIFS\_v3 score 100.0\% latency-SLO on \emph{every} (step, load) cell
        under both workloads, including 40 steps.  INFaaS's 30-step attainment
        is already variable with workload (93.4\% COCO vs.\ 100.0\%
        DiffusionDB), reflecting its fixed single-model routing rather than any
        prompt-content effect.  The systems that met the SLO under COCO captions
        continue to meet it under real prompts, and the systems that failed the
        SLO (Large-Only at 40 steps, INFaaS at 40 steps under stress) continue
        to fail it by the same margin.
  \item \textbf{Throughput and completion are workload-invariant.}  Mean
        throughput matches to within $\pm$0.001 req/s per system (Small-Only
        0.0192$\to$0.0197, INFaaS 0.0110$\to$0.0113, DIFS\_v3 0.0207$\to$0.0206,
        Large-Only 0.0054$\to$0.0054).  Every cell reports 100\% completion and
        zero dropped requests under \emph{both} workloads, and step-count
        accuracy SLO is 100.0\% throughout.  Prompt content is not a
        performance variable at fixed step count, model, CFG, and image size:
        compute per request is determined by steps and model, not by text.
  \item \textbf{Which system holds 100\% under both workloads?}  Exactly the
        same two: \textbf{DIFS\_v3} and \textbf{Clipper Small-Only}.  DIFS\_v3
        achieves this with 5 workers and step-count fair-share \emph{routing}
        rather than a single cheap model, which is the key behavioural
        difference retained under the DiffusionDB workload.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Chapter 6: Model-Switching Behaviour
% ============================================================
\chapter{Model-Switching Behaviour}

Every figure in this chapter is a v6 reproduction of the corresponding figure
sets from DIFS\_OC\_v4 (hot-switching overhead) and DIFS\_OC\_v5 (three worker
switch modes and their SLO context), generated from v6's \emph{own} measured
data and styled with the same colours and hatches as the reference projects,
so plots can be read side-by-side across the three writeups.  The worker
implements three selectable switch modes (environment variable
\texttt{DIFS\_MODEL\_SWITCH\_MODE}):

\begin{description}
  \item[hot] Cache pointer flip to an already-resident model.
  \item[force\_upload] Genuine DRAM $\to$ GPU weight re-upload plus sync; the
        target is staged in host memory, no disk read.
  \item[cold\_every] Always reload the target from disk into DRAM and then push
        to GPU; never reuse the cache.
\end{description}

Together with the cold \emph{initial} loads recorded during the R6 run
(\texttt{metrics/model\_load\_metrics.csv}), these modes define a three-tier
memory hierarchy for the scheduler: \textbf{GPU-resident (hot)} $\to$
\textbf{DRAM-resident (warm / force\_upload)} $\to$ \textbf{disk (cold)}.

\section{Measured switch costs}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{v6 measured switching costs: median over the 20 off-diagonal model
  pairs (range in parentheses), cost as a fraction of the fixed 60\,s latency
  SLO, and cost relative to the demand seen at the low and stress load levels
  (inter-arrival times of $\sim$30\,s and $\sim$6\,s).  Data:
  \texttt{switch\_<mode>/pairwise\_switching.csv} (this project) and
  \texttt{model\_load\_metrics.csv}.}
\label{tab:switch_costs}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Median} & \textbf{Range} & \textbf{\% of 60\,s SLO}
                 & \textbf{vs.\ 30\,s gap} & \textbf{vs.\ 6\,s gap} \\
\midrule
hot            & 0.010\,ms  & 0.010\,ms (flat) & $1.7\times10^{-5}$\%
                    & $\sim$0\% & $\sim$0\% \\
force\_upload  & 188.6\,ms  & 73.4--461.8\,ms  & 0.31 $\to$ 0.77\%
                    & $\sim$0.6\% & $\sim$3.1\% \\
cold\_every    & 3.79\,s    & 0.89--8.11\,s    & 6.3 $\to$ 13.5\%
                    & $\sim$12.6\% & $\sim$63\% \\
cold initial load & 1.0--6.7\,s (per model) & 0.70--10.6\,s
                    & 1.7 $\to$ 17.7\% & $\sim$3--22\% & $\sim$17--112\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The tiers are separated by roughly three orders of magnitude: hot is
$\sim$$19{,}000\times$ faster than force\_upload, which in turn is
$\sim$$20\times$ faster than cold\_every (medians 0.010\,ms, 189\,ms, 3.79\,s).
The biggest absolute costs are concentrated on the heaviest targets
(SD-3.5-Large, SD-3.5-Medium): median cold\_every cost to land on those targets
is 5.93\,s and 5.55\,s respectively, versus 1.0--1.1\,s for the two SD-v1-5/2-1
models; the same ordering holds for force\_upload (212\,ms and 163\,ms vs.\
73--124\,ms), confirming that the switch cost is dominated by the \emph{size of
the target weights}, not the identity of the source.

