\section{When to cold-switch vs.\ when to warm-switch}

The three measured tiers are not interchangeable; each occupies a different
place in the memory hierarchy, and the right choice follows from whether the
scheduler can \emph{resolve the target's residency} and how often the target
will be re-used.  The cost model from
Table~\ref{tab:switch_costs} gives the following decision framework.

\subsection{Best situations for cold switching}

Cold switching --- reloading the target from disk --- is the right choice when
the \emph{residency itself} is the problem, not the switch latency:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Scale-down and memory reclamation.}  Cold is the only mode
        that actually \emph{frees} memory: hot keeps weights in VRAM, warm keeps
        them in DRAM.  After a demand burst, under co-tenant GPU/DRAM pressure,
        or when the pool must be shrunk to a cheap subset, a cold reload is the
        only primitive that returns both DRAM and VRAM to the allocator while
        still being reversible on demand.  There is no warm alternative here, by
        construction.
  \item \textbf{Rare, long-lived demand.}  At the low/medium load levels the
        inter-arrival windows are 9000\,s/4500\,s.  A workload that touches
        SD-3.5-Large only a handful of times per hour should \emph{not} keep it
        resident for the whole epoch; the one-off 5.9--8.1\,s cold load (paid
        once) amortises to $\ll0.1\%$ of the epoch, whereas warm would waste
        $\sim$7\,GB of DRAM for the entire window on near-zero utilisation.
        Cold converts ``rarely needed'' into ``cheaply available''.
  \item \textbf{The heavy-quality tail.}  For 30--40-step requests on the large
        model, generation alone approaches the SLO, and the switch cost is a
        \emph{fraction} of the compute it unlocks.  An 8.1\,s worst-case cold
        switch is $\leq13.5\%$ of the 60\,s budget and well below one
        SD-3.5-Large generation; when the synchronous work after the switch is
        tens of seconds, the reload is second-order and cold is ``cheap
        insurance'' for a rare heavy request.
  \item \textbf{Correctness, versioning, and cold start.}  A cold reload
        guarantees clean weights and state: fresh checkpoints after training
        drift, no stale cache pointers, a reset CUDA context, and the only sane
        behaviour after a crash or at the very beginning of a run.  The
        1.0--6.7\,s initial loads already recorded in
        \texttt{model\_load\_metrics.csv} are the unavoidable cold component of
        \emph{every} run; planning for it (warm-up the pool before the first
        arrival) is mandatory regardless of the scheduler's runtime policy.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Best situations for warm (\texttt{force\_upload}) switching}

Warm switching --- staging the target in DRAM and re-uploading to GPU --- is
the right choice whenever \emph{the target can be kept (or prefetched) in host
memory} and the switch will happen reasonably often:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{High-frequency reconfiguration.}  At the high, saturation and
        stress levels the inter-arrival time falls to 10--6\,s.  A cold switch
        (median 3.79\,s) then blocks the worker for up to $\sim$63\% of one
        inter-arrival, whereas a warm switch (median 0.19\,s) blocks for
        $\sim$3\%.  Any scheduler that adapts the active model set on the
        timescale of a few requests must use warm for the inner loop; cold is
        catastrophic at this cadence.
  \item \textbf{Heavy-model rotation economics.}  The models with the largest
        cold penalties (SD-3.5-Medium/-Large: 5.6--5.9\,s per target median)
        are exactly the ones whose warm cost is smallest in relative terms
        (163--212\,ms, a $\times$$28$--$35$ saving).  This asymmetry is the
        strongest argument for warm: it lets the scheduler \emph{toggle the
        powerful models} in and out of the active set at sub-second cost,
        which is precisely the mechanism a 100\%-SLO policy needs when 40-step
        demand shifts onto SD-3.5.  A cold policy would either keep the heavy
        models permanently resident (wasting the very memory that motivated
        switching) or stall requests for $\sim$6--8\,s on every rotation.
  \item \textbf{SLO protection under tight budgets.}  Warm's worst pair
        (461.8\,ms) consumes 0.77\% of the 60\,s budget; cold's worst
        (8.11\,s) consumes 13.5\%.  In the latency-critical cells --- 40-step
        requests where inference already sits near the SLO --- an 8\,s stall
        can push a request over budget; a 0.46\,s stall cannot.  Systems that
        make \emph{hard} SLO guarantees should therefore run their switching on
        warm so the switch term is second-order and provable.
  \item \textbf{Deterministic cost bounds.}  Warm variance is tight
        (73--462\,ms), while cold's disk-bound spread is nearly
        $9\times$ (0.89--8.11\,s) and its initial-load spread wider still.
        Admission control and back-pressure need worst-case bounds; warm gives
        them.
  \item \textbf{Prefetch pipelining.}  A DRAM $\to$ GPU upload can be
        overlapped with an in-flight generation: stage the target during the
        current request so only the burst transfer stalls the pipeline.  The
        disk read of a cold switch cannot be hidden on a single worker.  Warm
        turns an apparent switch latency into a near-zero on-path stall, which
        is why it is the natural inner-loop primitive for a scheduler that
        already stages models ahead of time.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Choosing one over the other}

The decision is a \textbf{residency triage}, not a question of speed alone.
Prefer the \emph{shallowest tier whose residency you can afford}, using the
measured hierarchy as the cost ladder:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{GPU-resident $\to$ hot.}  If the target is already in VRAM,
        switching costs 0.01\,ms and there is zero reason to pay anything else.
        On this node the whole five-model pool fits the 49\,GB GPU, which is why
        a more \emph{hot-first} policy (DIFS\_v3) attains 100\% latency-SLO in
        R6 while paying a switch cost that is, by
        Figure~\ref{fig:overhead_fraction}, $10^{-7}$--$10^{-6}$ of the
        generation time.
  \item \textbf{DRAM-staged $\to$ warm.}  If the target is (or can be
        prefetched to) host memory, use force\_upload.  Warm is the correct
        default for \emph{adaptive} reconfiguration because it is
        $\sim$$20\times$ cheaper than cold and its cost is tight enough to
        guarantee SLOs.  Break-even rule from
        Table~\ref{tab:switch_costs}: as long as a target is re-used roughly
        every $20\,\textrm{s}$ or sooner, warm amortises its DRAM staging
        against the disk read that cold would pay each time.
  \item \textbf{Disk-only $\to$ cold.}  Use cold only when residency must be
        released (scale-down, eviction, co-tenancy), the artifact changed
        (checkpoint/version), or the model is being used for the first time in
        a run (cold start).  In the hot path of a high-rate workload, cold is
        not a choice but a last resort.
\end{enumerate}

Operationally, an SLO-serving scheduler should therefore run a \emph{two-tier
policy}: keep the frequently used models GPU-resident (hot), stage the
next-likely tier in DRAM (warm), and demote to disk (cold) only for evicted or
never-seen targets.  On this single-node geometry the measured outcome is
benign --- the flat 0.01\,ms hot matrix means switching is not a factor in the
R6 results (Chapter~7) --- but the same measurements quantify what v6's
scheduler would need if the working set ever exceeded residency: warm switching
preserves SLOs through $\sim$$300$ switches per budget, while a single cold
switch already spends up to a seventh of the request budget and would cap a
reconfiguration chain at $\sim$$7$--$16$ switches per request.

% ============================================================
% Chapter 7: Analysis and Interpretation
% ============================================================
\chapter{Analysis and Interpretation}

\section{Key findings}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Workload robustness.}  Replacing curated COCO captions
        (median $\sim$10 words) with 10{,}000 real DiffusionDB prompts
        (median $\sim$20 words, rich in artist/style/quality modifiers) changes
        no aggregate metric by more than $1.8$ percentage points of latency-SLO
        attainment (INFaaS 92.2$\to$94.0; Clipper Large-Only 74.3$\to$73.2) and
        no throughput by more than 0.0005 req/s.  The systems' \emph{ranking} is
        unchanged: DIFS\_v3 $>$ Clipper Small-Only $>$ INFaaS $>$ Clipper
        Large-Only on mean latency-SLO attainment (100, 100, 94.0, 73.2).
  \item \textbf{The discriminator is the model/step trade-off, not the
        prompt.}  The only cells that fail the 60\,s latency SLO are the ones
        served by SD-3.5-Large at 30--40 steps (Clipper Large-Only, 40-step
        collapse; INFaaS 40-step, pinned to SD-3.5) or that batch too much
        compute onto one model.  At a fixed step count the DiffusionDB prompt
        text adds zero measurable latency, because diffusion cost depends on
        steps $\times$ model, not on the prompt string.
  \item \textbf{No dropped work.}  Completion is 100\% under both workloads at
        every load level including stress (10 req/min), with zero dropped
        requests and zero accuracy-SLO violations---evidence that the pool and
        admission-control path absorb arrivals without loss under either
        prompt distribution.
  \item \textbf{Switching is not a factor here.}  Hot-switch times between all
        model pairs are a flat 0.01\,ms and the \emph{only} cold loads
        (medians 1.0--6.7\,s per model) are paid at run start; a fully resident
        pool therefore pays a per-switch cost of
        $10^{-7}$--$10^{-6}$ of generation time (Chapter~6).  The three measured
        switch tiers (hot / force\_upload / cold\_every) span four orders of
        magnitude and define when a 100\%-SLO policy must fall back from hot to
        warm and finally to cold switching.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Workload effect on scheduling decisions}

Prompt content exerts its influence through \emph{workload statistics} that the
scheduler observes---most notably the step-count mix (which step level a request
receives)---not through the raw text.  DIFS\_v3's fair-share step adaptation
kept 100\% latency-SLO at all five load levels under the DiffusionDB workload
because it continues to route 40-step requests onto models that can complete
them within 60\,s, exactly as it did under COCO.  The DiffusionDB pool's longer
prompts change the \emph{semantics} of generations but not the per-step compute
cost; this is why the SLO outcomes, throughput, and completions are
statistically indistinguishable from v4.  In a real service the practical
consequence is benign: serving policies tuned on curated captions remain valid
when users type long, stylised prompts.

\section{External validity of the v4 ablation}

v6 provides the external-validity check that a single curated dataset
(COCO captions) cannot offer.  The v4 conclusions---DIFS\_v3-style routing with
fair-share step adaptation meets the latency SLO at all loads; a Large-Only
policy collapses on 40-step requests; INFaaS routing degrades at 40 steps;
completion is upheld even under stress---were obtained with DiffusionsDB's
\emph{real user-generated prompts}.  Since the results are unchanged, the v4
ablation is not an artefact of the caption distribution, and the generalisation
claim ``DIFS\_v3 maintains 100\% latency-SLO attainment on a multi-model worker
pool over a broad range of request rates, for our model stack and step
allocations'' now rests on two independent workloads.

% ============================================================
% Chapter 7: Conclusions
% ============================================================
\chapter{Conclusions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{What v6 adds to the v4 ablation.}  It turns the v4 single-curated-workload
        study into a two-workload controlled comparison: identical hardware,
        codebase, baselines, load model, SLO cuts, and sampling procedure, with
        the only changed variable being the prompt distribution (10{,}000 real
        user prompts vs.\ 202{,}654 COCO captions).  v6 thereby isolates whether
        the v4 conclusions are artefacts of curated caption text.
  \item \textbf{Where DiffusionDB confirms v4.}  Every headline conclusion
        survives: DIFS\_v3 and Clipper Small-Only hit 100\% latency-SLO at all
        load levels; Clipper Large-Only collapses on 40-step requests (0\%
        both workloads); INFaaS degrades at 40 steps (75.9\% vs.\ 75.5\%);
        completion is 100\%, no requests dropped, step-count accuracy SLO 100\%
        under both workloads.  Throughput is invariant to prompt content
        ($\pm$0.001 req/s).
  \item \textbf{Where it diverges.}  Only in small, statistically explainable
        movements: Clipper Large-Only 30-step 97.1$\to$93.0 (Poisson sampling at
        low load) and INFaaS 30-step 93.4$\to$100.0.  No divergence changes the
        ranking or the deployment recommendation.
  \item \textbf{Limitations and future work.}  Single-node, single 49\,GB GPU,
        emulated Clipper/INFaaS baselines (not the original repos), one
        generation per request (no batching), fixed 5-worker pool, static
        step-count levels, and prompts sampled deterministically with seed 42
        from a 10{,}000-prompt pool.  Future work: real INFaaS/Clipper
        deployments as baselines, multi-GPU worker pools, larger step/cost
        spaces, prompt-length-stratified load, and batching over the heavy 40-step
        requests that cause today's only SLO failures.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
% Reproducibility
% ============================================================
\chapter{Reproducibility Checklist}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \texttt{pip install pyarrow} in the \texttt{DIFS\_Gen} env.
  \item Download \texttt{pooclub/diffusiondb} \texttt{metadata.parquet}
        ($\sim$190\,MB) to \texttt{results/diffusiondb\_data/}.
  \item \texttt{python src/scripts/build\_diffusiondb\_pool.py} rebuilds
        \texttt{annotations/diffusiondb\_prompts.json}.
  \item \texttt{python run\_baseline\_benchmark.py --baseline all}
        (crash-resilient; cells rerun individually on restart).
  \item \texttt{python src/experiments/collect\_pairwise\_switching.py}
        measures per-mode switch matrices under
        \texttt{DIFS\_MODEL\_SWITCH\_MODE=}\texttt{hot}/\texttt{force\_upload}/\texttt{cold\_every}
        into \texttt{switch\_<mode>/pairwise\_switching.csv}.
  \item \texttt{python compile\_report\_v6.py} regenerates all plots
        (baseline, workload-comparison, v4 switching-overhead, v5 switch-mode
        and switch-SLO), fills every table from v6 CSVs, and compiles the PDF
        (also reads v4 CSV).
\end{enumerate}

